{"name":"Blue Ballad","hex":"#7498bd","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"ballad","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A kind of narrative poem, adapted for recitation or singing; especially, a sentimental or romantic poem in short stanzas.","A slow romantic song."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make mention of in ballads.","To compose or sing ballads."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ballad"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ballad","blocks":[{"text":"A ballad is a form of verse, often a narrative set to music. Ballads were particularly characteristic of the popular poetry and song of England, Ireland and Scotland from the Late Middle Ages until the 19th century. They were widely used across Europe, and later in Australia, North Africa, North America and South America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Maria_Wiik_-_Ballad.jpg/330px-Maria_Wiik_-_Ballad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballad","wikidata":"Q182659"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Here_begynneth_a_gest_of_Robyn_Hode.png/330px-Here_begynneth_a_gest_of_Robyn_Hode.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Here_begynneth_a_gest_of_Robyn_Hode.png/330px-Here_begynneth_a_gest_of_Robyn_Hode.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A sixteenth-century printed ballad, the A Gest of Robyn Hode","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHere_begynneth_a_gest_of_Robyn_Hode.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Scott%27s_Minstrelsy_Of_The_Scottish_Border.JPG/500px-Scott%27s_Minstrelsy_Of_The_Scottish_Border.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Scott%27s_Minstrelsy_Of_The_Scottish_Border.JPG/1280px-Scott%27s_Minstrelsy_Of_The_Scottish_Border.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Scott%27s_Minstrelsy_Of_The_Scottish_Border.JPG/500px-Scott%27s_Minstrelsy_Of_The_Scottish_Border.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Walter Scott's Minstrelsy of the Scottish Border","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AScott's_Minstrelsy_Of_The_Scottish_Border.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5